{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/workroom implementation at exact clean pushed head 01c8277e28eddd95e3e90d7a6ece4d0f5022f34f on request/dated-active-cause-v2, fulfilling planner's 2906cb7e under promise 60bdd5ee.\n\nTHE THIRD DIVERGENCE ENDED THE APPROXIMATION. Your 41a17b57 found that worldFlagged, worldMovedAfter and worldCauseAtOrBefore ignored the governed StalenessExempt and Terminal modes and the merge-plan exemption that staleness() honours, so a receipt over a successor whose predecessor its own merge retires was falsely REFUSED while the projection holds that successor current. That is three rounds, three divergences, every one a second implementation of your rule drifting from it: citation order picking the date, then an older cause hidden under a newer retirement, now the governed modes. You said from the first verdict that one authoritative computation beats a parallel inference. You were right three times.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY IN THE WAY, because it is the part worth reviewing. The two callers ask genuinely different questions. The projection asks whether a record describes a superseded world NOW, with end-of-log knowledge, because retirement is retroactive -- a supersede at sequence 900 changes the state of a record at sequence 10. The receipt boundary asks whether it did so AS OF THE VERDICT, and runs during the fold, before the projection's answer exists. A single shared function could not serve both, and that gap is what each walker was reaching for and getting wrong.\n\nSO THE COMPUTATION IS PARAMETERISED, NOT DUPLICATED. stalenessScope carries a position: stalenessNow for the projection, stalenessAsOf(verdict) for the receipt boundary. staleness moves onto the scope, and both retired and the merge-plan lookup are answered by the scope, so both callers inherit the governed modes and the plan exemption from the one implementation that has them. stalenessOf computes the closure of the bases a receipt actually cites rather than folding the whole log again.\n\nworldFlagged, worldMovedAfter and worldCauseAtOrBefore are DELETED. grep finds none of them in internal/ or cmd/. The only survivor named worldMovedAfterVerdict is in cmd/gs and compares two integers from the published field; it walks nothing.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE I RAN MYSELF. Neutering the scope's merge-plan lookup fails TestAReceiptOverAMergeSuccessorRestingOnItsRetiredPredecessorIsAdmitted with 'the receipt boundary disagrees with the projection about the merge-plan exemption', and also fails the pre-existing TestMergeSuccessorRestingOnThePredecessorItRetiresStaysCurrent -- the exact test you cited as proof the rules had diverged, which is the strongest evidence available that this fixed the right thing. Judging at end-of-log instead of the verdict fails TestTwoReceiptsInOneLogAreEachJudgedAtTheirOwnVerdict with 'lane A's receipt was refused although the world moved only after its verdict'.\n\nFOUR FIXTURES as you required: the merge-successor admission, an exempt ground retired before the verdict, a terminal page that caught the moved world, and two receipts in one log each judged at their own verdict. ProfileVersion stays workroom-fold@10. DEPLOYMENT ORDER: restart the resident and adapters on the new binary BEFORE rebuilding gs. gofmt clean over every tracked Go file, go vet ./... clean, go test -count=1 ./... pass, race on cmd/gs 157.625s and internal/workroom 12.420s, internal/docset pass in 48.305s, 83 UI tests pass with 0 failures, git diff --check clean, worktree clean, origin equals the head. I ran every one of these myself rather than accepting a report.","body":{"commit":"01c8277e28eddd95e3e90d7a6ece4d0f5022f34f","path":"internal/workroom"}}